chaos cases exercised (each asserts all five pull-discipline rules hold):
  - partition: gateway drops pulls -> client retries 3x per rule 5, then
    PartitionError; server journal empty, audit unchanged, server never
    chases; healed partition serves the next pull normally.
  - replay: resent identical request -> identical sealed response bytes,
    duplicate flag set, exactly 1 data read and 1 EVIDENCE log entry
    (idempotent single effect); same req_id with different bytes ->
    sealed "req-id-reuse" denial.
  - clock skew: FakeClock +/-301s vs server -> "stale-request" sealed denial
    (seal verifies, DENIAL logged, chain verifies); +/-300s and 0 -> accepted.

key custody: Phase-0 TEST ceremony (CEREMONY-TEST marker asserted present in
every test); ceremony keys held only by the governance-plane component.
No production keys. No key bytes cross a plane boundary (seal/verify ops
take key names; key material never leaves governance).

spec decisions recorded (flagged, not invented — see final report):
  D1 response/denial sealed with the requesting surface's enrollment key
     (the only key a surface holds), sealed inside governance.
  D2 gateway transport envelope on server-initiated pulls uses a PUBLIC
     framing constant (TRANSPORT_KEY) — carries no authority; authority
     lives in the inner governance-sealed envelopes.
  D3 proof verification by a surface (which holds no master key) runs inside
     governance via the "verify" op, keyed by name.
  D4 denial returned as the sealed envelope itself (content carries
     req_id/reason/ts; seal top-level), not a flattened copy.
  D5 replay cache keyed on req_id + exact request bytes.
  D6 "server initiates nothing" verified by audit traceability: every
     gateway record carries its originator's req_id.
